WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
Correcting a measured pass rate.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:05.699
Here’s the payoff of measuring TPR and TNR separately.

3
00:00:05.979 --> 00:00:15.173
Once the judge is frozen, you run it on new outputs that nobody labeled, say last week’s production replies, and it reports an observed pass rate q.

4
00:00:15.453 --> 00:00:22.259
That number is biased by the judge’s errors: it counts failures the judge let through, and misses passes it flagged.

5
00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:24.885
If you know the error rates, you can undo that.

6
00:00:25.165 --> 00:00:27.683
Let p be the true pass rate on the new outputs.

7
00:00:27.963 --> 00:00:37.307
A truly passing output is called pass with probability TPR; a truly failing one is called pass with probability 1 minus TNR.

8
00:00:37.587 --> 00:00:41.853
So, assuming the judge’s error rates on new outputs match what you measured,.

9
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:47.637
q = TPR times p plus (1 minus TNR) times (1 minus p).

10
00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:49.283
This is linear in p.

11
00:00:49.563 --> 00:00:56.517
Collect the terms: q = p times (TPR plus TNR minus 1) plus (1 minus TNR).

12
00:00:56.797 --> 00:00:57.651
Solving,.

13
00:00:57.931 --> 00:01:03.584
p = (q plus TNR minus 1) / (TPR plus TNR minus 1).

14
00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:19.928
Epidemiologists have used exactly this estimator since Rogan and Gladen’s 1978 paper on estimating disease prevalence from an imperfect screening test, where TPR is the test’s sensitivity and TNR its specificity.

15
00:01:20.208 --> 00:01:22.533
Three properties follow from the algebra.

16
00:01:22.813 --> 00:01:27.955
A perfect judge (TPR = TNR = 1) leaves q unchanged.

17
00:01:28.235 --> 00:01:39.883
The denominator must be positive: if TPR plus TNR is 1 or less, the judge is no better than a coin weighted to its own pass rate and q carries no information about p.

18
00:01:40.163 --> 00:01:44.365
And sampling noise can push the estimate outside 0 to 1, so clip it.

19
00:01:44.645 --> 00:01:50.725
A worked example: the judge passes 79 percent of production replies, with TPR 0.

20
00:01:51.005 --> 00:01:52.776
